 " of them."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"c9844cbb-cbac-44d8-be39-438615646b2e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The concatenation of walks ",
 StyleBox["u = (u\:2081, \[Ellipsis], u\:2096)", "TI"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["w = (w\:2081, \[Ellipsis], w\:2098)", "TI"],
 " with ",
 StyleBox["u\:2096 = w\:2081", "TI"],
 " is ",
 StyleBox["u \[CenterDot] w = (u\:2081, \[Ellipsis], u\:2096, w\:2082, \
\[Ellipsis], w\:2098)", "TI"],
 ". Walks that do not meet end to start give nothing."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"3afccfd4-bd7f-4977-bab8-61f091466914"],

Cell["\<\
Each argument can be one walk or a family: a vertex list, a walk graph, a \
list of vertex lists or a list of walk graphs. Every walk of the first family \
is joined to every walk of the second that starts where it ends. The result \
is a list of directed path graphs.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"4012c2e9-cefc-4624-820e-25c7122e9345"],

Cell["\<\
The concatenation of two shortest paths need not be a shortest path. Joining \
the members of two segments at a common point gives the members of the \
polyline through the three points.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"0fce6700-c3e1-484f-afb4-c981d9ce01e7"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The count ",
 StyleBox["n", "TI"],
 " can be ",
